#!bovnar 1.1
# units.bvnr — single source of truth for all 163 physical base units.
#
# HOW THIS WORKS
#   Hand-edited. gen_units.py (at the repo root) regenerates the C unit tables
#   from this file on every build; never edit the generated *.gen.{h,inc}. To
#   add, remove, or change a unit, edit a record below and rebuild.
#
# STABLE IDS (ABI)
#   .id is the value_base_unit_t enum value. Append-only: NEVER renumber or
#   reuse an id — downstream C and the Python bindings are pinned to these
#   integers. Physical units occupy 1..133, 348..377 and 380..; the gap
#   134..347 belongs to currencies (see currencies.bvnr), as do 378..379 —
#   currencies appended after that gap was frozen. A new physical unit goes at
#   the end (380+) and must move the last-enumerator check in
#   src/utils/bvn_internal_dims.h with it.
#
# FIELDS (one record per unit)
#   .id       value_base_unit_t enum value (stable; see above)
#   .name     enumerator suffix; the C constant is  bu_<name>
#   .symbol   canonical OUTPUT symbol, real UTF-8 (e.g. "Ω", "°C")
#   .factor   to_si_factor: value of one unit in coherent SI (bare float)
#   .dims     SI dimension exponents as a NAMED struct with zeros omitted, so a
#             dimensionless unit is .dims = {}. Field names: length mass time
#             current temperature amount luminosity
#   .affine   true for offset scales (e.g. °C); else false
#   .offset   affine offset (bare float; 0.0 when .affine is false)
#   .prefix   prefix policy: default | info | german | ratio | none
#   .aliases  every accepted INPUT spelling, real UTF-8 (matched exactly);
#             include the canonical symbol when it is itself parseable
#
# COMPACT PREFIX FORM
#   A prefixed unit may be written with the '~' separator ("k~g") or compactly,
#   without it ("kg"). The compact form is resolved by the same longest-suffix
#   match as any other spelling, so a bare alias always wins over a prefixed
#   reading ("min" is the minute, never milli-inch) and no existing document can
#   change meaning. .compact_exceptions below lists the few compact spellings
#   that must stay a parse error anyway; see the comment on that list.

